import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.Ke88N3OB.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Code style","description":"Biome, the JSDoc rule, and how code is organized and tested","frontmatter":{"navigation":{"category":"Plugin development","type":"spotlight"},"title":"Code style","description":"Biome, the JSDoc rule, and how code is organized and tested","editLink":"https://github.com/filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins/edit/master/docs/code-style.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/code-style.md","filePath":"docs/code-style.md","lastUpdated":1792252051000}`),a={name:`guides/code-style.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="code-style" tabindex="-1">Code style <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-style" aria-label="Permalink to “Code style”">​</a></h1><p>Use this guide when you add or change code in the repository. Run <code>pnpm lint</code> to check your changes; CI runs the same command. Biome handles formatting and general linting, while ESLint checks public code for JSDoc descriptions.</p><h2 id="formatting-and-linting" tabindex="-1">Formatting and linting <a class="header-anchor" href="#formatting-and-linting" aria-label="Permalink to “Formatting and linting”">​</a></h2><p><a href="https://biomejs.dev" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Biome</a> formats and lints the files listed in <code>biome.json</code>. Run <code>pnpm lint:fix</code> to apply automatic fixes; it also formats SVG files with Biome&#39;s HTML formatter. <code>pnpm lint</code> checks without changing files. If you use the <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/agent-tooling">agent check runner</a>, it checks only changed files and does not apply fixes, so it can run safely while other local edits are present.</p><p>Follow the rules in <code>biome.json</code>: use single quotes, four spaces for indentation, shorthand array types such as <code>T[]</code>, and <code>import type</code> for type-only imports. Explicit <code>any</code> is an error.</p><h2 id="jsdoc" tabindex="-1">JSDoc <a class="header-anchor" href="#jsdoc" aria-label="Permalink to “JSDoc”">​</a></h2><p>Biome doesn&#39;t check documentation, so ESLint uses <code>eslint-plugin-jsdoc</code> in <a href="../eslint.config.mjs"><code>eslint.config.mjs</code></a> to require a description for public code. Add a JSDoc description to exported functions, classes, interfaces, type aliases, enums and constants, and to public methods of exported classes. Explain what the code is for; don&#39;t just repeat its name.</p><p>TypeScript already provides parameter and return types, so you don&#39;t need <code>@param</code> or <code>@returns</code> tags. Add them when they explain something the signature can&#39;t, such as units, defaults or what an error means. If you add a <code>@param</code> tag, its name must match a real parameter. Don&#39;t include types in JSDoc tags.</p><p>The rule skips tests (including <code>test/</code> and <code>testing/</code> directories), config files and the <code>turbo/generators/templates/</code> tree. Private helpers don&#39;t need descriptions. It checks <code>.ts</code> source across the repository and <code>.mts</code> and <code>.mjs</code> source in <code>tools/static-hermes-runtime</code>. Keep declaration files aligned with their source API; this rule doesn&#39;t lint them.</p><h2 id="color-in-console-logs" tabindex="-1">Color in console logs <a class="header-anchor" href="#color-in-console-logs" aria-label="Permalink to “Color in console logs”">​</a></h2><p>Format values in console log messages with <code>ansi.named</code> from <a href="./../tools/minecraft-colors/README"><code>@pumpkin-plugins/minecraft-colors</code></a>. Its terminal colors use the same named roles as <code>color.named</code>, which formats Minecraft codes for chat replies. Leave the surrounding message plain instead of adding formatting codes at the log call site.</p><p>Keep this palette aligned with the Pumpkin server version pinned in <a href="../tools/test-harness/src/pumpkin-version.ts"><code>tools/test-harness/src/pumpkin-version.ts</code></a>. Pumpkin uses cyan for plugin names, green for versions and bold yellow for permissions in its <a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Pumpkin/blob/0.2.0%2B26.3-26.51/crates/pumpkin/src/plugin/mod.rs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">plugin permission prompt</a>. Its <a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Pumpkin/blob/0.2.0%2B26.3-26.51/crates/pumpkin/src/logging.rs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">rolling log file layer</a> strips ANSI codes before writing saved logs. Review these sources when the pinned server changes; update the named roles if its palette or log handling changes.</p><h2 id="structure" tabindex="-1">Structure <a class="header-anchor" href="#structure" aria-label="Permalink to “Structure”">​</a></h2><p>Keep each file focused on one job. If a file starts doing two things, look for a useful seam and split it; around 150 lines is a prompt to review the structure, not a hard limit.</p><p>Keep core logic separate from Pumpkin and WASI. Put logic such as parsing, ordering and protocol state machines in plain modules, and pass their dependencies in. Put Pumpkin- or WASI-specific work behind a thin adapter such as <code>DataFiles</code> or <code>Transport</code>. That keeps most code easy to unit test and lets real-server tests focus on the boundary with Pumpkin and WASI.</p><p>Group a plugin&#39;s <code>src/</code> files by concern, such as <code>config/</code>, <code>packs/</code>, <code>web/</code> or <code>platform/</code>, rather than by file type. When more than one plugin needs the same code, put it in a package under <code>tools/</code> as <code>@pumpkin-plugins/config</code> does; don&#39;t copy it between plugins.</p><h2 id="tests" tabindex="-1">Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests" aria-label="Permalink to “Tests”">​</a></h2><p>Put unit tests (<code>*.test.ts</code>) beside the code they cover. Put real-server tests (<code>*.itest.ts</code>) in <code>test/</code>, with one file per concern. The <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/testing">Testing guide</a> covers how to run them.</p><p>Test behavior, including failure paths such as invalid input, missing files and clients that hang up. There&#39;s no need to assert behavior that TypeScript&#39;s types already guarantee. Generate fixtures in code—for example, build <code>.mcpack</code> archives in the test—instead of committing binary files.</p><h2 id="package-metadata" tabindex="-1">Package metadata <a class="header-anchor" href="#package-metadata" aria-label="Permalink to “Package metadata”">​</a></h2><p>When you add or update a package, run <code>node scripts/package-metadata.mjs</code> to check its metadata. The script checks the license, homepage, repository, bugs field and a description of at most 70 characters. It derives workspace links from the root repository URL. Add <code>--fix</code> to write values that can be derived; the script leaves descriptions and package-specific fields for you to maintain.</p><p>For public npm tools, set <code>engines.node</code> from <code>.node-version</code>. Tools that consumers import set <code>sideEffects</code>. If a tool has a package-root entry, set top-level <code>module</code> and <code>types</code>; if it exposes only subpath APIs, declare <code>types</code> and runtime conditions on each public entry in <code>exports</code>. Each tool also owns its <code>files</code> and <code>publishConfig</code> settings.</p><p><code>pnpm gen</code> creates plugins that pass these checks. A package&#39;s homepage points to its folder README on <code>master</code>. Packages use MIT by default. If a package has its own <code>LICENSE</code>, <code>LICENSE.md</code> or <code>LICENSE.txt</code> file, keep the license declared in its <code>package.json</code>; <code>--fix</code> preserves that value.</p><h2 id="keep-docs-in-sync-with-code" tabindex="-1">Keep docs in sync with code <a class="header-anchor" href="#keep-docs-in-sync-with-code" aria-label="Permalink to “Keep docs in sync with code”">​</a></h2><p>If a code change makes a doc inaccurate, update the doc before you finish. <code>pnpm fix</code> checks release configuration, fixes derived package metadata, regenerates READMEs, checks documentation and ends with <code>pnpm lint:fix</code>. You still need to correct release registration and errors in handwritten docs yourself.</p><p>The documentation checks catch different kinds of drift:</p><ul><li><code>pnpm readme:check</code> checks generated README content, including plugin docs, the root action and package tables, and action input and output tables generated from <code>action.yml</code>.</li><li><code>pnpm check</code> verifies release configuration and package metadata, then runs <code>scripts/check-docs.mjs</code>. That script checks for missing package READMEs, broken links, headings and repository paths, invalid <code>pnpm</code> commands, pages missing from the docs index, undocumented root scripts and CI jobs missing from the CI table.</li></ul><p>Keep plugin documentation tied to the code that drives it. Declare commands once with <code>defineCommands</code>, then use that tree for registration and README command rows; it also constrains command permission nodes to the plugin name. <code>PluginInfo</code> checks requested permission names against Pumpkin&#39;s <code>PumpkinPermission</code> type, including <code>sys.env.&lt;NAME&gt;</code>. Define settings in the config schema and pass its <code>describeConfig</code> result to <code>info.config</code>, so defaults, validation and README settings come from the same source. See <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/plugin-info-and-readmes">Plugin info and READMEs</a> and <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/plugin-config">Plugin config</a> for these contracts.</p><p>Scripts can&#39;t tell whether prose still describes the behavior. Read the docs you touched and the ones that explain your change. When you rename something, search <code>docs/</code> and the READMEs for the old name; the pull request template also asks you to confirm that the prose is accurate.</p>`,29)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};